test("#902 · HOME_WORK_PACK_CLIENT is not HOME_ONBOARDING_CLIENT and mints no id any other lane owns", () => {
  // A POSITIVE CONTROL on the fixture itself: the two client ids `home-board-mock.mjs` mints
  // must differ, or #902's own scoping would resolve to the SAME row #650's onboarding cell reads.
  const homeBoard = readFileSync(join(E2E_DIR, "home-board-mock.mjs"), "utf8");
  const onboardingMatch = /HOME_ONBOARDING_CLIENT = \{\s*id: "([0-9a-f-]+)"/.exec(homeBoard);
  assert.ok(onboardingMatch, "home-board-mock.mjs must still declare HOME_ONBOARDING_CLIENT's id in this shape");
  assert.notEqual(HOME_WORK_PACK_CLIENT.id, onboardingMatch![1]);
});
```
The title says '…and mints no id any other lane owns', but the body only compares against ONE other id, `HOME_ONBOARDING_CLIENT`, inside the SAME file (`home-board-mock.mjs`). Nothing here scans any other lane's mock. This is the same finding the SPEC axis already recorded as L01-S7 (note severity, no action required); it is re-surfaced here specifically as a documented-standards matter because AGENTS.md rule 6 ('claims need evidence … no evidence, no claim') applies to a test's own name exactly as it applies to prose: a reader trusting this title would believe a cross-lane id collision is guarded against here, and it is not — a future lane minting the same id in a different `*-mock.mjs` would not red this cell.
